{"InteractionName":{"value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drug_interactions/DB00176_DB00182","type":"uri"},"drugbankDrug2":{"value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00182","type":"uri"},"IntEffect":{"value":"Risk of serotoninergic syndrome","type":"literal"},"drugbankDrug":{"value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00176","type":"uri"},"Drug":{"value":"http://dbpedia.org/resource/Fluvoxamine","type":"uri"},"description":{"value":"Fluvoxamine (brand name as Luvox��, Faverin��, Fevarin�� and Dumyrox��) is an antidepressant which functions pharmacologically as a selective serotonin reuptake inhibitor. Though it is in the same class as other SSRI drugs, it is most often used to treat obsessive-compulsive disorder.\nFluvoxamine has been in use in clinical practice since 1983 and has a clinical trial database comprised of approximately 35,000 patients. It was launched in the US in December 1994 and in Japan in June 1999. As of the end of 1995, more than 10 million patients worldwide have been treated with fluvoxamine.","type":"literal"},"structure":{"value":"1","type":"literal"},"casRegistryNumber":{"value":"http://bio2rdf.org/cas:54739-18-3","type":"uri"}},
